"Figure caption: Schematic of library screening of LNPs for gene-editing protein delivery.LNP library design using DLin-MC3-DMA as the ionizable lipid, cholesterol, and DMG–PEG-2000, with six helper lipids at varied ratios. In vitro screening in HEK Ai9 cells is used to identify top-performing formulations. Top-performing LNPs are clustered by helper lipid charge into eight groups (five formulations per group) for in vivo evaluation. The top formulation is then selected and evaluated for gene knockout efficiency using CCR5 or PD-1 protein in vivo.\n\nFigure caption: HTS for RNP LNPs with HEK Ai9 cells and ML analysis.(A) Gene-editing efficiencies of 486 RNP LNPs were evaluated after 48-hour incubation by quantifying the percentage of tdTom+ cells using flow cytometry (n = 3). The gating strategy is shown in fig. S2. (B) The top 45 formulations were selected on the basis of a combined metric of the percentage of live tdTom+ cells among the total cells using CRISPR-Max as the control group. The cell viability is shown in fig. S3. (C) SHAP analysis plots of feature importance on output from RNP LNP ML models. Dots represent unique LNPs, with color indicating the relative feature value for each LNP. The location on the x axis defines the feature value’s impact on model predictions, with values toward the right indicating enhanced gene-editing outcomes. (D) LNP library gene editing visualized in two-dimensional (2D) space after t-stochastic neighbor embedding (t-SNE) of SHAP values. Each dot represents a unique LNP, and the normalized transfection efficiency is scaled between 0 and 1 on the basis of the minimum and maximum transfection in the dataset.\n\nFigure caption: In vivo screening of T cell–targeting RNP LNP formulations.(A) In vivo cluster-based screening of RNP-loaded LNPs. Eight clusters were tested (50 μg per mouse via intravenous injection), and gene-editing efficiency was assessed by measuring tdTom+ immune cells, including T cells, B cells, CD11b+ myeloid-like cells, and CD11c+ DC-like cells, in the spleen via flow cytometry at 7 days postinjection. The gating strategy is shown in figs. S4 and S5. (B) Gene-editing efficiency of individual formulations within the selected clusters (A, B, and C) was evaluated by quantifying tdTom+ T cells (CD45+ CD3+ cells) in the spleen using flow cytometry at 7 days postinjection (50 μg of RNPs per mouse via intravenous injection). (C) Immune cell–type distribution of transfected tdTom+ immune cells in the spleen following treatment with formulation F48, analyzed by flow cytometry. Cell types examined include T cells, B cells, CD11b+ myeloid-like cells, and CD11c+ DC-like cells. (D) Gene-editing efficiency of F48 RNP–loaded LNPs in various cell types and organs (spleen, liver, and lungs) was assessed by flow cytometry at 7 days postinjection. (E) Representative confocal images of the spleen at 7 days postadministration of F48 RNP–loaded LNPs, stained with DAPI (blue), CD45 (cyan-blue), CD3 (green), and tdTomato (red). Scale bars, 50 and 20 μm. The tile-scanned image showing a larger spleen structure is shown in fig. S9. Data represent means ± SEM from an experiment [n = 3 biologically independent samples for (A) to (C); n = 7 biologically independent samples for (D)].\n\nFigure caption: In vivo assessment of functional gene knockout of CCR5 and PD-1.(A) Schematic illustration of mouse model treatment for CCR5 knockout. C57BL/6 mice were given one intravenous (iv) injection of F48 LNPs loaded with 50 μg of RNPs (sgCCR5) on day 0 and day 7. Mice were bled at 5 and 10 days postinjection, and the spleens were extracted on day 15 (n = 4). (B) Flow cytometry quantification of CCR5 levels on CD45+CD3+ and CD45+CD3+CD4+ cells in the blood on days 5 and 10 and in the spleen on day 15 (n = 4). CCR5 expression levels were normalized to the mean value of the untreated control group (100%). The gating strategy in fig. S10. (C) Levels of CCR5 protein expression in lymphocytes from blood on days 5 and 10 and in the spleen on day 15, quantified using Western blot. α-Tubulin was used as a loading control. Band intensities quantified and normalized to α-tubulin. OD, optical density. (D) Schematic illustration of the mouse model treatment of PD-1 knockout. C57BL/6 mice were immunized with the Moderna LNPs loaded with mOVA intramuscularly (i.m.), followed by one intravenous injection of F48 loaded with 50 μg of RNPs (sgPD-1) on day 14. Spleens were extracted at 10 days postinjection (n = 6). (E) Flow cytometry quantification of PD-1 levels on CD45+CD3+ and CD45+CD3+CD8+ splenocytes at 7 days postinjection (n = 4). PD-1 expression levels were normalized to the mean value of the untreated control group (100%). The gating strategy is shown in fig. S12. (F) Levels of PD-1 protein expression in spleen lymphocytes on day 15, quantified through Western blot (n = 6). α-Tubulin was used as a loading control. Band intensities quantified and normalized to α-tubulin. Data were analyzed using an unpaired t test. *P < 0.05; **P < 0.01; ***P < 0.001; ****P < 0.0001.\n\nFigure caption: In vivo tumor suppression and PD-1 knockout by LNP-delivered sgPD-1 RNPs in a B16-OVA melanoma model.(A) Schematic of the experimental timeline. C57BL/6 mice were inoculated with 3 × 105 B16-OVA tumor cells on day 0. On days 4 and 11, mice received 10 μg of mOVA formulated with the Moderna LNPs (i.m.). On days 5 and 12, treatment groups received sgPD-1 RNP LNPs (50 μg of RNPs, intravenously), anti–PD-1 antibody (50 μg, intraperitoneally), or PBS. Tumors were extracted for analysis on day 18 (n = 5). (B) Tumor growth curves for all mice in this study. (C) PD-1 expression on CD45+CD3+ and CD45+CD3+CD8+ tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes. Gating strategy shown in fig. S16. Statistical significance of tumor volume was analyzed on day 17 between two ICB treatment groups using unpaired t test (P = 0.000567). (D) Representative flow cytometry plots of CD8+ TILs showing reduced PD-1+ populations labeled with allophycocyanin (APC; percentage indicated) in sgPD-1 RNP LNP–treated mice. Data represent means ± SEM. Statistical significance was determined by two-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) with Tukey’s multiple comparisons test. n.s., P > 0.05; *P < 0.05; **P < 0.01; ***P < 0.001.\n\nFigure caption: HTS for Cre recombinase-loaded LNPs with HEK Ai9 cells and ML analysis.(A) Gene-editing efficiency of 486 Cre-loaded LNPs was assessed after 48 hours by quantifying the percentage of tdTom+ cells using flow cytometry (n = 3). (B) The top 50 formulations were selected on the basis of combined metric, the percentage of living tdTom+ of total cells with DOTAP-10 serving as the reference LNP control. The cell viability is shown in fig. S17. (C) SHAP summary plots (left) depicting the impact of various features on ML model predictions. Each dot represents a unique LNP with color indicating the relative feature value. The x-axis position denotes the feature’s effect on model predictions, with values toward the right correlating with enhanced gene-editing outcomes. On the right, a 2D visualization of the LNP library is generated via t-SNE of SHAP values, where each dot represents a unique LNP, and normalized transfection efficiency is scaled between 0 and 1. (D) In vivo cluster-based screening of Cre-loaded LNPs. Ten clusters were tested (50 μg of Cre per mouse via intravenous injection), and gene-editing efficiency (tdTomato+ immune cells) in the spleen was measured by flow cytometry at 7 days postinjection. (E) Gene-editing efficiency of individual formulations within the selected cluster was evaluated by quantifying tdTom+ immune cells in the spleen via flow cytometry. (F) Immune cell–type distribution of transfected tdTom+ immune cells in the spleen after treatment with formulation F53, analyzed by flow cytometry. Cell types examined include DC-like cell, myeloid-like cells, T cells, and B cells. Data represent means ± SEM from an experiment [n = 3 biologically independent samples for (A), (B), (E), and (F); n = 4 biologically independent samples for (D)].\n\nFigure caption: Biodistribution and cellular uptake profiles of selected LNP formulations for RNPs and Cre recombinase.(A) Biodistribution at 6, 24, and 48 hours (hr) post-intravenous injection of selected RNP LNPs (50 μg of RNPs per mouse encapsulated in Cy5-labeled DMG-PEG LNPs) in C57BL/6 mice, assessed by IVIS. Data are expressed as the percentage of total radiant efficiency across individual organs. (B) Cellular uptake in spleens at 6, 24, and 48 hours post-intravenous injection of F48 RNP LNPs, assessed through flow cytometry. Data expressed as the percentage of each population within the Cy5+ immune cells. (C) Cellular uptake of F48 in spleens at 6, 24, and 48 hours post-intravenous injection. The percentage of Cy5+ cells was quantified in each immune cell population with flow cytometry. (D) Hydrodynamic size of the selected F48 formulation with sgAi9 RNPs, measured using dynamic light scattering (DLS). Data represent the intensity-weighted size distribution of the nanoparticles. (E) Biodistribution at 6, 24, and 48 hours post-intravenous injection of F53 (50 μg of Cre protein per mouse encapsulated in Cy5-labeled DMG-PEG LNPs) in C57BL/6 mice, assessed by IVIS. Data are expressed as the percentage of total radiant efficiency across individual organs. (F) Cellular uptake in spleen at 6, 24, and 48 hours post-intravenous injection of F53 Cre LNP, assessed through flow cytometry. Data are expressed as the percentage of each population within the Cy5+ immune cells. (G) Cellular uptake of RNP LNPs in spleens at 6, 24, and 48 hours post-intravenous injection. The percentage of Cy5+ cells was quantified in each immune cell population using flow cytometry. (H) Hydrodynamic size of the selected F53 Cre LNPs measured using DLS. Data represent the intensity-weighted size distribution of the LNPs. Data represent means ± SEM from an experiment; n = 4 biologically independent samples for (A) to (C) and (E) to (G)."